      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f1384c08-dd64-54bd-b3c4-df507895ab64",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: M. S. Swaminathan, Norman Borlaug, Gregory Conko\n- Topics: Green Revolution in India, genetically modified food, high-yielding crop varieties\n- Plants: wheat, Onobrychis viciifolia, alfalfa, clover\n- Awards: Nobel Peace Prize, Presidential Medal of Freedom, Congressional Gold Medal\n\nFacts:\n- During the Green Revolution, higher-yielding varieties of wheat became the main development in India.\n- Many scientists developed these higher-yielding wheat varieties, including Indian geneticist M. S. Swaminathan and American agronomist Dr. Norman Borlaug.\n- During the Green Revolution, Onobrychis viciifolia was replaced by high-yielding alfalfa and clover species.\n- Norman Borlaug received multiple honors for his work, including the Nobel Peace Prize, the Presidential Medal of Freedom, and the Congressional Gold Medal.\n- In The Frankenfood Myth, Gregory Conko argues against over-regulation of genetically modified food.\n- The Frankenfood Myth features a foreword by Nobel Peace Prize-winner Norman Ernest Borlaug.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
